course: books
lesson: 84
title: Just So Stories, How the First Letter Was Written, part 1: a broken spear and a picture on birch-bark
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Long, long ago, in the Stone Age, a man called Tegumai lives in a Cave with his wife Teshumai and his little daughter Taffy. Nobody in the family can read or write, and they do not mind. Taffy goes everywhere with her Daddy and they come home happy and «shocking dirty».
explain: One day they go fishing for carp in the Wagai river, miles from home, and Tegumai breaks his spear on the river bottom. Mending it will take half the day. Taffy wishes they could write, so they could send a message home for Daddy's other spear, the big black-handled one.
explain: A Stranger-man from a far tribe comes along. He does not understand a word of their language. Taffy tries to tell him to go and fetch the spear; he hands her a piece of birch-bark to show he means no harm, and she thinks he wants her Mummy's address.
explain: Taffy borrows the shark's tooth from his necklace and scratches a picture on the bark: Daddy with the broken spear, the black-handled spear, herself explaining, the Stranger-man holding the spear, two trees, a hill, the beaver-swamp with beavers' heads, the Cave, and Mummy with her hands up.
explain: But the Stranger-man reads the picture quite differently. He thinks Tegumai must be a great Chief in danger, with enemies all around, and that the child is asking him to call the whole tribe to help.
fact: «Neolithic» means New Stone Age, when people made tools of stone and began farming; Kipling says Tegumai «lived cavily in a Cave» and «wore very few clothes».
fact: The names and their meanings: Tegumai Bopsulai, «Man-who-does-not-put-his-foot-forward-in-a-hurry»; Teshumai Tewindrow, «Lady-who-asks-a-very-many-questions»; Taffimai Metallumai, «Small-person-without-any-manners-who-ought-to-be-spanked», called Taffy.
fact: Teshumai says: «Really, my Tegumai, you're no better than my Taffy.»
fact: Tegumai's spear is wood with shark's teeth at the end; he breaks it «clean across» by jabbing too hard on the river bottom, and he has forgotten to bring extra spears.
fact: Taffy offers to run home for the other spear, but Tegumai says it is too far, and she might fall into the beaver-swamp on the way.
fact: «Here's a pretty kettle of fish!» means here's a nice mess; «make the best of a bad job» means do what you can when things go wrong.
fact: His «mendy-bag» holds reindeer-sinews (tough strings from an animal's body), strips of leather, bee's-wax and resin (sticky tree-gum).
fact: Taffy says it is «an awful nuisance» that they cannot write; her Daddy tells her not to use slang, as «Awful» isn't a pretty word.
fact: The Stranger-man belongs to a far tribe and does not understand one word of their language; he answers only «Um!» He gives Taffy birch-bark to show «his heart was as white as the birch-bark», meaning he meant no harm.
fact: The Stranger-man believes his shark's tooth is magic and that anyone who touches it without leave will «swell up or burst»; when Taffy does not, he decides she is a very wonderful child.
fact: Because Tegumai never turns round, the Stranger-man thinks he must be a haughty, great Chief, «Man-who-turns-his-back-on-callers».
fact: Taffy draws lying flat on her tummy with her legs in the air. She draws only the beavers' heads, because she says she can't draw beavers, and she draws Mummy with her hands up because Mummy will be so pleased to see the Stranger-man.
fact: In the picture the black-handled spear «looks as if it was sticking in Daddy's back», because the tooth slipped and the bark was too small.
fact: Real life: people did not write in the Stone Age. Writing was invented about 5,000 years ago, first in Mesopotamia (now Iraq) and Egypt, and later, separately, in China and in Central America.
fact: Real life: pictures are much older than writing. People painted animals in caves tens of thousands of years ago, but a picture is not yet writing: it does not tell you the exact words, so it can be read in different ways, just as here.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bare» (London) = very, lots of; «delulu» = believing something silly that isn't true; «wee» (Scotland) = small; «fixin' to» (southern USA) = about to.
exercise: Where does Tegumai's family live? | In a Cave | In a hut by the Wagai river | In a tree-house in the forest | In a tent made of reindeer skin
exercise: What does «Tegumai Bopsulai» mean? | Man-who-does-not-put-his-foot-forward-in-a-hurry | Man-who-catches-the-biggest-carp-in-the-river | Lady-who-asks-a-very-many-questions | Man-who-mends-spears
exercise: What does Taffy's long name mean? | Small-person-without-any-manners-who-ought-to-be-spanked | Little-girl-who-draws | Best-Beloved-of-all | Small-person-who-asks-a-very-great-many-questions-all-day
exercise: What does Teshumai say when they come home dirty? | «You're no better than my Taffy» | «Wash your feet before you come in, both of you» | «Where is the fish you promised me?» | «Off to bed, both of you, at once!»
exercise: Why do Tegumai and Taffy go to the Wagai river? | To spear carp-fish for dinner | To fetch water for Mummy | To find beavers for the Tribe | To meet the Stranger-man, who lives on the other bank
exercise: What is Tegumai's spear made of? | Wood with shark's teeth at the end | Stone with a bone handle | Iron with a sharp point, bought from traders | Reindeer horn
exercise: How does Tegumai break the spear? | By jabbing it too hard on the river bottom | By hitting a rock while running | A carp bit it in two | Taffy sat on it by accident while they were eating lunch
exercise: «Here's a pretty kettle of fish!» What does Tegumai mean? | Here's a nice mess | Look at all these fish | Let's cook our carp in a kettle | What a beautiful river this is, full of fine fish
exercise: Why won't Tegumai let Taffy run home for the other spear? | It is too far, and she might fall into the beaver-swamp | Mummy has gone away for the day to visit the Neolithic ladies in the other caves | The spear is too heavy | She doesn't know the way
exercise: What does Tegumai keep in his «mendy-bag»? | Reindeer-sinews, leather strips, bee's-wax and resin | Fish-hooks, nets and a little bag of lunch | Paint, brushes, beads and a spare shark-tooth necklace for luck | Shark's teeth only
exercise: What word does Tegumai tell Taffy not to use, because it is slang? | «Awful» | «Nuisance» | «Dreadful» | «Horrible»
exercise: Why can't the Stranger-man help at first? | He doesn't understand a word of their language | He is deaf | He is in a great hurry to get home to his own little girl-daughter | He is afraid of the river
exercise: What does the Stranger-man say when Taffy asks if he knows where her Mummy lives? | «Um!» | «Yes, follow me» | «Ask your father» | «Humph!»
exercise: Why does the Stranger-man give Taffy birch-bark? | To show his heart was white as the bark and he meant no harm | To ask her to draw him a map of the river and the swamp | Because it was his lunch | To help mend the spear
exercise: What does Taffy think the bark is for? | He wants Mummy's address | He wants her to eat it up for lunch | He wants her to make a boat | He wants her to throw it in the river for luck
exercise: What does Taffy use to scratch her picture? | The shark's tooth from the Stranger-man's necklace | A sharp stone | Her Daddy's broken spear | A burnt stick that Daddy brought from the fire at home
exercise: What does the Stranger-man believe about his shark's tooth? | That anyone touching it without leave would swell up or burst | That it brings good fishing | That it was a gift from the Chief | That it can talk
exercise: In which position does Taffy draw? | Lying flat on her tummy with her legs in the air | Sitting on her Daddy's shoulders, high above the river | Standing on a rock | Kneeling in the water
exercise: Why does the spear in the picture look as if it is sticking in Daddy's back? | The shark's tooth slipped and the bark wasn't big enough | Taffy wanted to frighten her Mummy into hurrying | She drew it on purpose | The Stranger-man joggled her elbow
exercise: How does Taffy draw the beavers? | Only their heads, because she can't draw beavers | As big as the hills, all with spears in their paws | As little dots | She leaves them out
exercise: Why is Mummy drawn with her hands up? | Taffy knows she will be pleased to see the Stranger-man | Mummy is frightened by enemies | Mummy is reaching up to take the spear down from over the fireplace | Mummy is waving goodbye
exercise: Looking back: which earlier tale also had a hero with a knife who was «a man of infinite-resource-and-sagacity»? | The Whale story, with the shipwrecked Mariner | The Camel story, with the Man who leaves the Camel alone | The Leopard story | The Kangaroo story
test: Zara: «Taffy could write, she just didn't feel like it, delulu teacher said otherwise.» (delulu = believing something silly) Put it right. | Nobody in the family could read or write; Taffy can only draw | Right, Taffy wrote a long letter in neat letters on the birch-bark | Taffy could write but had no bark | Only Teshumai could write
test: Why does the Stranger-man think Tegumai is a great Chief? | Tegumai never turns round or notices him, as if too grand | Tegumai wears a crown of carp-scales and feathers | Taffy tells him so | Tegumai speaks his language
test: Tegumai says «we must make the best of a bad job». What does he do? | Mends the spear himself | Goes straight home to the Cave | Gives up fishing for good and walks home sulking | Sends Taffy to fetch help
test: What would have happened if the Stranger-man had understood Taffy's language? | He could simply have been told to fetch the spear | He would have refused to help, being from another tribe | He would have drawn a picture | Nothing different
test: Which is TRUE about Taffy's picture? | It shows Daddy, both spears, Taffy, the Stranger-man, the way home and Mummy | It shows only one thing, the black-handled spear, drawn very large in the middle | It is written in letters | It is painted in colours
test: Dev from Glasgow: «wee Taffy drew it all perfect, every beaver.» (wee = small) Put it right. | She only drew the beavers' heads, and says she can't draw them | Right, every beaver was there with its tail and teeth | She drew no beavers at all | She drew just one big beaver
test: Real life: when and where was writing first invented? | About 5,000 years ago, in Mesopotamia and Egypt | In the Stone Age caves of Britain, by a little girl and her father one summer morning | About 500 years ago, in England | Ten thousand years ago in Australia
test: Real life: why is a picture not yet writing? | It does not show the exact words, so people can read it differently | Pictures are always too small and too faint to see properly | Pictures cannot show people | Pictures are newer than writing
test: Real life, bare (= very) old: which came first in human history? | Cave pictures, tens of thousands of years before writing | Writing, which came long before anybody ever drew a single picture | Both on the same day | Printed books
test: Looking back: the Stranger-man believes a magic tooth will make people burst. Which earlier character also believed something that was not so? | Painted Jaguar, who thought the curled-up Hedgehog was the Tortoise | The Elephant's Child, who believed his nose would shrink back if he only waited | The 'Stute Fish | Baviaan
test: Looking back: Tegumai's daughter is called «Best Beloved» here. Whom does Kipling call «O Best Beloved» all through the book? | The child listening to the stories | Taffy only, and nobody else in any story | The Camel | Painted Jaguar
test: Looking back: like Taffy, which earlier hero was always asking questions? | The Elephant's Child, full of «'satiable curtiosity» | Old Man Kangaroo, who asked three gods to make him different | The Whale | The Hedgehog
test: Looking back: the Elephant's Child set off with a hundred pounds of bananas. What does Tegumai carry in his mendy-bag? | Sinews, leather strips, bee's-wax and resin | Nothing, he forgot it | A hundred pounds of bananas | A whole carp
test: Ellie from Alabama: «Taffy was fixin' to run home herself.» (fixin' to = about to) Why didn't she? | Daddy said it was too far and she might fall in the beaver-swamp | She was scared of the Stranger-man and wanted to stay near her Daddy | She had hurt her foot | It was raining hard
test: ★★★ Careful: the Stranger-man reads the spear «sticking in Daddy's back» as an attack. What does the story tell us really caused it? | The tooth slipped and the bark was too small | Taffy drew a battle on purpose | Tegumai asked her to draw it | The beavers had attacked
test: ★★★ Taffy's name means «Small-person-without-any-manners...». Her mother's means «Lady-who-asks-a-very-many-questions». Which of these is shown in THIS part? | Taffy takes the Stranger-man's magic tooth without leave | Teshumai asks the Stranger-man many questions | Tegumai hurries | Taffy is very polite
test: ★★★ The Stranger-man thinks the picture means «enemies are coming». Which of Taffy's drawings did he most likely take for an army? | The beavers' heads in the swamp | The two trees | Mummy's raised hands | The hill
exam: Identify the material upon which Taffy executed her drawing. | A flat piece of birch-bark | A tablet of soft clay, subsequently baked | The smooth stone wall of the Cave | A deerskin, scraped and stretched
exam: Ascertain the reason for which Tegumai was unable to continue fishing. | His spear had been broken by being jabbed upon the river bottom | His daughter had frightened away every fish | The river had overflowed | A stranger had taken his spear
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Stranger-man's comprehension of the picture? | He supposed the Chief to be beset by enemies and in need of his tribe | He understood perfectly that a spear was required and set off directly for the Cave | He could not see it | He believed it to be a gift
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the invention of writing in the real world. | It arose about five thousand years ago, first in Mesopotamia and Egypt | It was invented by a single child of the Stone Age on the banks of a river in Surrey | It arose a thousand years ago in England | It has existed as long as human beings
